// THE MEMO FRAME. `frame` names every occurrence id recorded since the innermost memoized
// production started: a mint is one FrameMinted, and a memo hit or miss inside the frame is one
// FrameNested sharing the inner frame's list (a snoc, never a copy). A memo hit replays exactly
// its stored frame into the current index instead of merging the stored index whole. That is
// exact for every consumer: a node the hit returns carries only ids minted or replayed inside
// the memoized frame, so every id it can look up is in the replayed frame; entries the stored
// index held from before the frame belong to a different parse path, no node on this path
// references them, and the span index is read only by lookup(id).
type ParseProvFrameItem
  = FrameMinted { id: OccurrenceId }
  | FrameNested { frame: List<ParseProvFrameItem> }

type ParseProvenanceState {
  alloc: OccurrenceIdAllocator
  index: v2.std.provenance.SpanIndex
  frame: List<ParseProvFrameItem>
}

// THE ALLOCATOR RIDES BESIDE THE INDEX BECAUSE THEY ARE ONE FACT. `span_index` says where each
// occurrence this parse minted came from; `alloc` says which ids this parse consumed, so the NEXT
// parse in the same source graph can continue rather than restart. `std.occurrence_identity`
// `occurrence_identity_scope_law` names "module-local allocator reset" a FORBIDDEN identity input
// and requires that "a production source graph threads one allocator across every parsed source" --
// a per-file reset makes two files' occurrence #3 the same value with different meanings, which is
// the meaning fork of DESIGN section 3 at the identity layer, and it is what makes the ingest-wide
// span index unbuildable: merging two files' indices would silently take one file's locus for the
// other file's node. Carrying the high-water mark here is what lets the assembly fold seed each
// file from the previous file's next id, so an occurrence id is unique across the whole ingest and
// the join from a node to its file is a LOOKUP.
//
// A STANDALONE PARSE IS STILL AN ISOLATED SCOPE, which the same law admits: the unseeded entry
// points below are projections that pass `occurrence_id_allocator_initial()`, so a caller that
// parses one file alone is unchanged.
//
// WHAT THAT DOES NOT SAY, DELIBERATELY, IS THAT EVERY UNSEEDED CALLER PARSES ONE FILE. The door
// cannot enforce its own precondition: an unseeded call states "this is a fresh scope", and whether
// that is TRUE is a fact about the caller, not about this declaration. An earlier revision of this
// annotation claimed the corpus had no multi-file unseeded caller, and the corpus did have one
// (review 69644) -- prose asserting a property of code it cannot see, which is the same class as
// review 69607 against this branch. The obligation belongs to each fold that walks an ingest, and
// the two that do -- `v2.compiler.program_assembly` `program_assembly_fold_step` and
// `v2.compiler.00_compile` `native_test_context_fold_step` -- both thread the mark. A NEW ingest
// fold that calls the unseeded door is a defect this annotation cannot catch; making that
// structurally unwritable would mean a carrier that distinguishes "a scope" from "a file", which is
// a modeling change larger than this one and is not claimed here.
type ParseArtifact {
  tree: ParseTree
  span_index: v2.std.provenance.SpanIndex
  alloc: OccurrenceIdAllocator
}

data authored_parse_artifact_note: String = "The parse-layer twin of LexArtifact: the semantic parse result PLUS what the annotation channel captured, now bound to subjects. `authored_parse_semantic` is the erasure boundary — a consumer projecting through it sees exactly the ParseArtifact it saw before this channel existed, which is what lets every existing semantic consumer stay untouched while annotation-aware machinery reads the whole thing.

It carries NO refusal field. Refusals do not travel inside this artifact, because a wrapper holding both an annotation graph and a list of things that failed to attach invites a consumer to read the first and never the second.

The intended discipline is that this artifact exists only when nothing refused, and AuthoredParseOutcome below is the only thing in this module that constructs one. That is a CONVENTION, not yet a constraint: this is a plain record, so any module can construct one directly with whatever graph it likes. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: restrict construction to this module (sole_constructor or equivalent) with a control proving an outside construction does not compile — at which point this paragraph says `cannot` instead of `does not`."

data authored_parse_artifact_v2_roster_disposition: String = "SCAFFOLD — v2 carries the carrier, not yet the producer. Attachment needs a module-item subject roster (identity plus span per module-scope declaration), and the v2 parse tree does not project one today; the working attachment realization is the v1 seed parser, which is the production path that compiles this corpus. So the rows below are constructed and projected by execution, but no v2 parse entry point yet emits one with a non-empty graph.

The v2 lexical capture row also does not yet carry `preceded_by_blank_line`, the blank-line adjacency fact the shared attachment fold reads to decide where one authored block ends. It is deliberately absent rather than defaulted: v2 constructs no NormalizedAnnotationCapture today, so a field with no producer would be a value nobody computed, and a hardcoded `false` would silently merge blocks the author separated. The v2 walk can observe it from the same line-prefix state it already threads, and that lands with the producer below.

DISSOLVE-ON: v2 parse projects a module-item roster of AnnotationSubject from its parse tree, AND its capture row carries blank-line adjacency observed by the walk. At that point parse_module gains an authored sibling that calls std.source_annotation.attach_annotations — the SAME fold the v1 realization calls, never a second one — and this disposition row is deleted."

type AuthoredParseArtifact {
  semantic: ParseArtifact
  annotations: SourceAnnotationGraph
}

data authored_parse_outcome_note: String = "Refusal BLOCKS continuation; it does not ride along inside a successful-looking artifact. An earlier shape carried the refusals as a field on the wrapper, which left every consumer free to read `annotations` and never look at `refusals` — prose dropped, compilation proceeding, exactly the outcome admitting `//` and discarding the capture would have produced.

Only the accepted arm holds an AuthoredParseArtifact, so along this function's path `has annotations` and `nothing was refused` are the same fact rather than two fields a caller must remember to check together. That equivalence holds for artifacts this function produces; it is not enforced against a caller that constructs the record itself (see the carrier note above). The rejected arm still carries the semantic ParseArtifact, because the parse itself succeeded and the diagnostic must be reported against real located structure."

type AuthoredParseOutcome
  = AuthoredParseAccepted { artifact: AuthoredParseArtifact }
  | AuthoredParseRejected {
      semantic: ParseArtifact,
      refusals: FreeSemigroup<AnnotationAttachmentRefusal>
    }

fn authored_parse_outcome(
  semantic: ParseArtifact,
  attachment: AnnotationAttachmentResult
) -> AuthoredParseOutcome {
  match admit_annotations(result: attachment) {
    AnnotationsAdmitted { graph: graph } =>
      AuthoredParseAccepted {
        artifact: AuthoredParseArtifact { semantic: semantic, annotations: graph },
      }
    AnnotationsRefused { refusals: refusals } =>
      AuthoredParseRejected { semantic: semantic, refusals: refusals }
  }
}

fn authored_parse_semantic(artifact: AuthoredParseArtifact) -> ParseArtifact {
  artifact.semantic
}

fn authored_parse_annotations(artifact: AuthoredParseArtifact) -> SourceAnnotationGraph {
  artifact.annotations
}

type ParseMintSourceResult {
  id: OccurrenceId
  prov: ParseProvenanceState
}

type ParseStampResult {
  node: Node
  prov: ParseProvenanceState
}

type ParseMemoStats {
  hits: Int
  misses: Int
  lookup_calls: Int
}

// THE FURTHEST POSITION A REJECTED ALTERNATIVE REACHED, which is where a refusal is located.
// `parse_g0_tokens_remain` used to report the FIRST LEFTOVER token: the start of the module item
// whose production failed as a whole, after `repeat` stopped. That names the symptom item, not
// the refusal -- a `test fn` whose body holds one unadmitted construct was reported at `test`
// (DESIGN section 6b). The rejection that went furthest into the stream is the earliest token no
// admitted production could consume, so it is the locus. A join over one monotone order: the later
// token wins, end of input beats every token, and the empty value is the unit -- so the POSITION
// is independent of the order alternatives were tried in.
//
// A POSITION MAY CARRY ONE EXPECTATION: the reason of a `v2.std.grammar` `Expect` whose element
// began exactly there and failed. Only the position decides which record survives; at a tie the
// expectation recorded FIRST in parse order wins (ordered choice tries left before right), so the
// label is deterministic, and a later plain failure at the same position never erases it.
type ParseFurthestFailure
  = FurthestNone
  | FurthestAtToken { token: Token, expectation: Optional<ParseRefusalReason> }
  | FurthestAtEnd { expectation: Optional<ParseRefusalReason> }

type ParseMemoEntry
  = ParseMemoAccepted {
      remaining: List<Token>,
      captured: Node,
      diagnostics: List<Diagnostic>,
      prov: ParseProvenanceState,
      furthest: ParseFurthestFailure,
      refusal: Optional<Diagnostic>
    }
  | ParseMemoRejected { diagnostics: List<Diagnostic>, furthest: ParseFurthestFailure, refusal: Optional<Diagnostic> }

type ParseExprResult
  = ParseExprAccepted {
      remaining: List<Token>,
      captured: Node,
      diagnostics: List<Diagnostic>,
      prov: ParseProvenanceState,
      table: ParseTable
    }
  | ParseExprRejected { diagnostics: List<Diagnostic>, table: ParseTable }

type ParseSyncState {
  found_sync: Bool
  result: List<Token>
}

type ParseRepeatAcc {
  remaining: List<Token>
  acc_nodes: List<Node>
  acc_diags: List<List<Diagnostic>>
  done: Bool
  prov: ParseProvenanceState
  table: ParseTable
}

type GrammarExprCornerFold {
  corners: List<Symbol>
  nullable: Bool
}

type ParsePositionKey {
  position: Int
  production: Symbol
}

type ParseTableRealization {
  artifact_kind: std.cache_identity.ArtifactKindId
  provider_id: std.cache_identity.CacheInterfaceId
  materialization: Materialization
  grammar_digest: Hash
  token_stream_digest: Hash
  entries: Map<ParsePositionKey, ParseMemoEntry>
  memo_hits: Int
  memo_misses: Int
  memo_lookup_calls: Int
  furthest: ParseFurthestFailure
  nullable_set: List<Symbol>
  first_rows: Map<Symbol, List<FirstKey>>
  productions_by_name: Map<Symbol, GrammarProduction>
  prepared_exprs: Map<Symbol, PreparedGrammarExpr>
  layout: Optional<StreamLayout>
  refusal: Optional<Diagnostic>
}

type ParseTable = ParseTableRealization

type PreparedModeledGrammar {
  validated: ValidatedGrammarRoot
  analysis: GrammarFirstAnalysis
  overlap_residue_stage: Optional<NonEmptyParseStageDiagnostics>
}

type PreparedGrammar
  = PreparedVoidGrammar
  | PreparedModeled { modeled: PreparedModeledGrammar, materialization: Materialization }

data prepared_grammar_carrier_note: String = "PreparedGrammar is the once-per-grammar carrier: well-formedness, the five grammar_validate_for_parse checks, and the FIRST/nullable analysis (GrammarFirstAnalysis) are pure functions of the grammar, so prepare_grammar runs them ONCE and parse_module_prepared reuses the result per module. Previously parse_module re-ran the whole 5-check validation AND compute_grammar_first_analysis was computed twice (once in the ambiguity check, once in parse_table_for_production) on EVERY parse. Hoisting prepare_grammar above the assemble fold makes a door call over a K-module closure validate once, not K times — correctness-by-construction (the prepare sits above fold_list), no timing gate. The cache-interface fields on ParseTableRealization (grammar_digest/provider_id/materialization) stay for the cross-call content-addressed memo, which is gated on wiring the currently Miss-stubbed cache backend."

fn parse_grammar_not_well_formed_diagnostic(at: Locus) -> Diagnostic {
  Diagnostic {
    reason: ^parse_grammar_not_well_formed,
    at: port_refusal_locus(at: at, port: ^parse_stage_locus_port, invariant: ^parse_grammar_not_well_formed),
    correction: Unavailable { reason: ExternalContractUnknown }
  }
}

// Located at the furthest rejection when one reached past the first leftover token; otherwise at
// that token. A furthest record at end of input locates at the last token's end, zero-width.
// When the furthest record carries a grammar expectation, the refusal IS that expectation: its
// reason, located at the token the expected element was to follow (the last leftover token before
// the furthest position). An expectation whose anchor is not among the leftover tokens cannot be
// located on the leftover item, so the generic reason stands, located as before.
fn parse_g0_tokens_remain_diagnostic_at(tokens: List<Token>, furthest: ParseFurthestFailure) -> Diagnostic {
  match list_at_optional(xs: tokens, index: 0) {
    Absent => parse_g0_tokens_remain_diagnostic(tokens: tokens)
    Present { value: first } =>
      match furthest {
        FurthestNone => parse_g0_tokens_remain_diagnostic(tokens: tokens)
        FurthestAtToken { token: tok, expectation: expectation } =>
          if tok.start > first.start {
            match expectation {
              Present { value: reason } =>
                match parse_token_before(tokens: tokens, start: tok.start) {
                  Present { value: anchor } => parse_expectation_diagnostic(reason: reason, anchor: anchor)
                  Absent => parse_g0_tokens_remain_diagnostic(tokens: [tok])
                }
              Absent => parse_g0_tokens_remain_diagnostic(tokens: [tok])
            }
          } else {
            parse_g0_tokens_remain_diagnostic(tokens: tokens)
          }
        FurthestAtEnd { expectation: expectation } =>
          match list_at_optional(xs: tokens, index: length(xs: tokens) - 1) {
            Absent => parse_g0_tokens_remain_diagnostic(tokens: tokens)
            Present { value: last } =>
              match expectation {
                Present { value: reason } => parse_expectation_diagnostic(reason: reason, anchor: last)
                Absent =>
                  parse_g0_tokens_remain_diagnostic(
                    tokens: [Token { class: last.class, lexeme: last.lexeme, file: last.file, start: last.end, end: last.end }]
                  )
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

// The last token that ends at or before `start`: the token an expected element was to follow.
fn parse_token_before(tokens: List<Token>, start: Int) -> Optional<Token> {
  fold_list(
    xs: tokens,
    empty: optional_absent(),
    cons: fn(acc, tok) { parse_token_before_step(acc: acc, tok: tok, start: start) }
  )
}

fn parse_token_before_step(acc: Optional<Token>, tok: Token, start: Int) -> Optional<Token> {
  if tok.end <= start {
    optional_present(value: tok)
  } else {
    acc
  }
}

// THE GRAMMAR-AUTHORED REFUSAL. `reason` is a variant of the closed parse-stage vocabulary
// (`v2.std.parse_refusal_reason`), refusing under its declared diagnostic identity, and the locus
// is the source span of the token the expected element was to follow.
fn parse_expectation_diagnostic(reason: ParseRefusalReason, anchor: Token) -> Diagnostic {
  Diagnostic {
    reason: parse_refusal_reason_symbol(reason: reason),
    at: source_port_locus(
      port: ^parse_stage_locus_port,
      file: anchor.file,
      extent: ByteRange { start: anchor.start, end: anchor.end }
    ),
    correction: Unavailable { reason: UserInputBoundary }
  }
}

fn parse_g0_tokens_remain_diagnostic(tokens: List<Token>) -> Diagnostic {
  match list_at_optional(xs: tokens, index: 0) {
    Present { value: token } => Diagnostic {
      reason: ^parse_g0_tokens_remain,
      at: source_port_locus(
        port: ^parse_stage_locus_port,
        file: token.file,
        extent: ByteRange { start: token.start, end: token.end }
      ),
      correction: Unavailable { reason: UserInputBoundary }
    }
    Absent => Diagnostic {
      reason: ^parse_g0_tokens_remain,
      at: invariant_port_locus(
        port: ^parse_stage_locus_port,
        invariant: ^parse_tokens_remain_reported_without_a_first_token
      ),
      correction: Unavailable { reason: ExternalContractUnknown }
    }
  }
}

